<p>A database constraint makes a rule part of accepting a change. A second current reservation with the same external reference is refused even when it comes from an import or a staff tool that never saw the booking form. The useful question is which rule the database actually checks: a valid reference can still point across the wrong tenant boundary, and two different reservation numbers can still describe a duplicate.</p>
<p>We will extend the organisation booking service from <a href="/review/relational-next/data-design/">Designing relational data</a>. Alongside customers booking class places, an organisation now reserves rooms. Each room reservation names a customer and a resource in that organisation, has an external reference and occupies an interval. This is a separate <code>reservations</code> table: it does not change the parent’s policy of retaining one class booking per customer–session pair.</p>
<p>The SQL uses PostgreSQL 18. We will make each rule concrete with changes that pass and fail, then identify what still needs a protected transaction or an application decision. The figures are explanatory models; the recorded SQL checks ran on native PostgreSQL 18.6.</p>

