<h2 id="state-the-rule-before-choosing-its-constraint">State the rule before choosing its constraint</h2>
<p>“A reservation must be valid” leaves too much open. Our first rule is: every reservation names an existing customer and room in its tenant, records a non-empty external reference and has finite start and end instants, with the start before the end. Its state is draft, confirmed or cancelled. Initially, an external reference identifies one reservation within its tenant, including after cancellation.</p>
<p>Split that sentence by what must be compared. Required fields concern one value. The interval check compares two fields of one row. Uniqueness compares rows. References compare the reservation with other tables. Later, “confirmed reservations for one room must not overlap” will compare intervals across a subset of rows.</p>
<table><caption>Make the acceptance boundary observable</caption><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Accepted change</th><th>Rejected change</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><td>A positive-duration, finite interval</td><td>10:00 to 11:00</td><td>11:00 to 11:00; missing end</td></tr>
  <tr><td>One external reference per tenant</td><td>Tenant 2 also uses R17</td><td>A second R17 in tenant 1</td></tr>
  <tr><td>The room belongs to the reservation’s tenant</td><td>Tenant 1 reserves its room 21</td><td>Tenant 1 uses tenant 2’s room 29</td></tr>
  <tr><td>No overlapping confirmed room reservations</td><td>One ends when the next begins</td><td>Both occupy room 21 at 10:30</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Specify when each rule must hold too. These required values and interval bounds hold for drafts as well as confirmed reservations. The overlap rule applies only to confirmed rows. A reference in a circular relationship may need to hold at the end of the transaction. “Only an authorised organiser may cancel” concerns who may perform an operation; a list of allowed stored states does not enforce that permission.</p>
<p>Application validation can explain an invalid interval before submission. The database still needs its rule because there are other writers, and a previously valid proposal can become invalid before it is stored. Constraint names such as <code>reservation_interval</code> also give the application a stable way to recognise a known rejection without treating every error as the same problem.</p>

<h2 id="close-the-gaps-in-required-values-and-identity">Close the gaps in required values and identity</h2>
<p>Here is the first schema, for an empty disposable database. The customer and room tables are deliberately small; the important part is their tenant-scoped identity. <code>reservation_id</code> is a generated row number. The tenant’s external reference is an additional business key.</p>
<Sql code={schema} label="First reservation schema: required fields, interval, business identity and references" />
<details><summary>Load the small room-reservation fixture</summary><Sql code={fixture} label="Two tenants, their customers and rooms, and tenant 1’s confirmed R17" /></details>
<p>Generating a new number makes two stored rows distinguishable. It does not show that they represent different reservations. Without <code>UNIQUE (tenant_id, external_ref)</code>, two requests could store R17 under reservation numbers 1 and 2. Under the initial policy, the unique, required pair is also a candidate key: neither the tenant nor the reference alone identifies a row across all tenants.</p>
<p><code>NOT NULL</code> closes a different gap from <code>CHECK</code>. A comparison with a missing value generally produces unknown, and PostgreSQL accepts a check whose result is true or unknown. <code>CHECK (starts_at &lt; ends_at)</code> alone would therefore let a missing endpoint through. The required columns and the check together express the intended rule. The <code>isfinite</code> tests reject the special infinite timestamps as well.</p>
<p>A default supplies a value when the insert omits that column or explicitly asks for <code>DEFAULT</code>. It does not replace an explicitly supplied <code>NULL</code>. Omitting <code>state</code> produces <code>draft</code>; supplying <code>NULL</code> fails the requirement; supplying <code>lost</code> fails the state check. The same separation matters in an update.</p>
<table><caption>Missing values need their own tests</caption><thead><tr><th>Definition</th><th>Two rows with tenant 1 and a missing reference</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><td><code>UNIQUE (tenant_id, external_ref)</code>, reference nullable</td><td>Both accepted in PostgreSQL</td><td>Missing references do not compete by default</td></tr>
  <tr><td><code>UNIQUE NULLS NOT DISTINCT (tenant_id, external_ref)</code></td><td>Second rejected</td><td>At most one missing reference for that tenant</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Our required reference with ordinary uniqueness</td><td>Both rejected by <code>NOT NULL</code></td><td>Every reservation has a reference</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>These are different policies, not three spellings of the same key. Nor does required text mean useful text: our check rejects an empty reference, but a string of spaces is still present. Decide which strings identify the same external reference before enforcing their uniqueness. <a href="/review/relational-next/types/#preserve-identity-and-define-text-equality">Choosing types and representing missing values</a> owns text equality, input conversion and the meaning of missing data.</p>

<h2 id="reference-the-whole-relationship">Reference the whole relationship</h2>
<p>Suppose tenant 1 submits a reservation for resource 29, which belongs to tenant 2. Tenant 1 exists and resource 29 exists. Checking those facts independently would still allow the wrong relationship.</p>
<p>Figure 1 holds the reservation’s tenant fixed. Step through the cross-tenant case, then choose room 21 at the same step. Compare the resource number with the complete pair.</p>
<ConstraintFigure kind="reference" number={1} />
<p>The foreign key uses <code>(tenant_id, resource_id)</code> on both sides. Its target is the resource table’s composite primary key. The customer reference follows the same pattern. A missing pair fails on insert or update; changing or removing a referenced parent is governed by its reference action, which we will choose below.</p>
<p>Optional references need a policy for partially missing pairs. PostgreSQL’s default <code>MATCH SIMPLE</code> skips a composite reference check if any referencing column is null. In the small table below, <code>MATCH FULL</code> instead permits either both fields missing or a complete matching pair; <code>(1, NULL)</code> fails.</p>
<Sql code={optional} label="An optional pair must be wholly absent or wholly present" />
<p>Do not add <code>MATCH FULL</code> mechanically to a shared, required tenant column. If the booking’s own <code>tenant_id</code> is never null, an optional <code>referrer_id</code> cannot make that whole pair null. Default matching plus a nullable referrer can express “no referrer recorded; otherwise an existing customer in this tenant”. For a relationship with several optional components, require them together with an explicit row check or use a separately nullable pair as above. PostgreSQL 18 does not implement <code>MATCH PARTIAL</code>.</p>
<p>A foreign key constrains rows that carry a reference. It does not require every resource to have a reservation, or every reservation to have a participant. The empty room and empty child set remain valid unless another rule forbids them. Likewise, a generic <code>(target_type, target_id)</code> pair cannot be an ordinary foreign key that switches between the customers and resources tables. Separate relationships, or a genuinely shared entity table with suitable identity, can restore an enforceable reference.</p>

<h2 id="enforce-uniqueness-only-where-the-rule-applies">Enforce uniqueness only where the rule applies</h2>
<p>The service now changes its policy. It keeps cancelled attempts, but their external references may be reused. A reference must identify at most one <strong>current</strong> reservation per tenant, where current means draft or confirmed. “Current” is a stored-state definition here; the database is not deciding whether a timestamp has passed.</p>
<p>The old unique constraint is too strong: it compares cancelled rows too. In a fresh design, replace it with a <strong>partial unique index</strong>, a uniqueness check over rows matching its predicate.</p>
<Sql code={current} label="Allow cancelled attempts while keeping current references unique" />
<p>Figure 2 starts with one current R17. Try adding another, then retaining a cancelled R17. Finally select <em>Cancel then replace</em> and watch the old row leave the current set before the new one claims its reference.</p>
<ConstraintFigure kind="current" number={2} />
<p>The predicate is part of the rule. Reactivating a cancelled row or changing another current row’s reference to R17 must fail while a current R17 exists. Cancellation releases the reference; a new tenant may use it independently. A null reference still fails <code>NOT NULL</code>, whether current or cancelled.</p>
<p>Cancellation followed by replacement belongs in one transaction so a later failure does not leave the cancellation committed on its own. For the fixture with customer (1, 7), room (1, 21) and an existing current R17, the sequential change is:</p>
<Sql code={replace} label="Cancel and replace as one complete change" />
<p>The partial index is immediate; inserting the replacement first would fail. PostgreSQL cannot use this partial key as a foreign-key target, because it does not uniquely identify every stored reservation. Other tables should refer to the stable reservation ID, or the design can separate current reference ownership from retained attempts. That structural choice may simplify several rules at once; <a href="/review/relational-next/normalisation/#check-what-can-still-be-enforced-locally">normalisation and enforceability</a> examines the trade.</p>
<p>The SQL shows the rule on a disposable schema. Replacing a live constraint also means finding existing violations and coordinating old and new application behaviour. <a href="/review/relational-next/live-changes/">Changing a live database</a> owns that rollout.</p>

<h2 id="decide-when-related-changes-must-become-valid">Decide when related changes must become valid</h2>
<p>Suppose a group reservation must name a lead participant, and the lead must be one of <em>that reservation’s</em> participants. If the participant row points to the reservation and the reservation points back to its lead, neither row can be inserted first under two immediate references.</p>
<p>First ask whether the cycle is necessary. If the lead’s details can live on the reservation, a separate row may add no useful fact. If a reservation is allowed to exist before a lead is assigned, the real rule may apply only when it becomes confirmed. Here we deliberately choose the stronger rule: every committed reservation in this extension has a lead participant of its own.</p>
<p>This isolated extension makes that rule explicit. Its three-column lead reference prevents a participant from another reservation satisfying it. Only that reference is deferred; the participant’s reference back to its reservation remains immediate.</p>
<Sql code={lead} label="A required lead, constrained to be one of this reservation’s participants" />
<p>Step through Figure 3 with <em>Deferred · add lead</em>. Then omit the lead at the same step and continue to commit. Finally compare the immediate path.</p>
<ConstraintFigure kind="lead" number={3} />
<Sql code={cycleChange} label="A valid complete transaction and a final state that still fails" />
<p>A <strong>deferred constraint</strong> postpones a check; it does not make the final condition optional. <code>SET CONSTRAINTS reservation_lead IMMEDIATE</code> can bring its pending check forward and fails if the target is still missing. <code>NOT NULL</code> still requires a lead identifier at the first insert. Merely deferring the participant-to-reservation foreign key would not enforce the requirement that every reservation has a participant.</p>
<p>Check the particular engine and constraint. PostgreSQL can defer foreign keys, primary-key and unique constraints, and exclusion constraints; it cannot defer ordinary <code>CHECK</code> or <code>NOT NULL</code> constraints. A constraint declared deferrable but initially immediate normally checks at statement end until changed for the transaction. Non-deferrable uniqueness is stricter: PostgreSQL checks as individual rows are changed. The standalone partial unique index above is not a deferred constraint.</p>
<p>Deferral solves the order of this complete change. It does not establish how two sessions interact or how the application should retry a failure. Those questions belong to <a href="/review/relational-next/transactions/">Making concurrent changes correct</a>.</p>

<h2 id="make-reservation-conflicts-a-stored-rule-where-possible">Make reservation conflicts a stored rule where possible</h2>
<p>A confirmed reservation for room 21 from 10:00 to 11:00 conflicts with another confirmed reservation for that room from 10:30 to 11:30. Uniqueness on the two timestamps would miss it because neither endpoint pair is equal.</p>
<p>Use half-open intervals, written <code>[start, end)</code>: the start is occupied and the end is not. One reservation may therefore end at exactly 11:00 while the next starts then. Our finite, required bounds and strict start-before-end check reject empty, reversed and missing intervals before overlap has to be considered.</p>
<p>PostgreSQL’s <strong>exclusion constraint</strong> can reject a pair when all the listed comparisons hold. Here those comparisons are equal tenant, equal room and overlapping timestamp ranges. Its predicate includes only confirmed reservations.</p>
<Sql code={overlap} label="Forbid overlapping confirmed intervals for the same tenant and resource" />
<p><code>tstzrange</code> constructs the timestamp interval, and <code>&amp;&amp;</code> tests overlap. The <code>btree_gist</code> extension supplies GiST equality support for our integer tenant and room identifiers; range overlap is supported by the range type. This is a PostgreSQL mechanism, requiring the extension to be available in the deployment.</p>
<p>Figure 4 compares one candidate with an existing 10:00–11:00 reservation. Change its start to 11:00, then put it in another room. Finally make the overlapping candidate a draft and predict what would happen when it becomes confirmed.</p>
<ConstraintFigure kind="overlap" number={4} />
<p>The rule rejects an overlapping insert, a draft’s confirmation, a move into an occupied room or an endpoint update that creates a conflict. A draft can overlap; confirming it later must pass the check then. Neither a different tenant nor a different room conflicts under this rule. An adjacent interval passes, but equal start and end fail the separate interval check.</p>
<p>PostgreSQL 18 also has <code>WITHOUT OVERLAPS</code> on range-based temporal keys. The explicit exclusion form is useful here because our rule applies only to a state-defined subset. Verify support and restrictions on the actual engine; a feature with a similar name is not enough evidence of the same guarantee. <a href="/review/relational-next/history/#define-time-boundaries-and-correction-rules">Modelling history</a> chooses what historical intervals mean.</p>
<p>If the engine cannot express this rule declaratively, carry the same invariant and boundary cases into transaction design. An availability query is still useful to show choices, but “read no conflict, then insert” leaves a gap for another writer. The transaction branch decides how that gap is protected. The figure is a comparison model, not a demonstration of locks or a measured simultaneous execution.</p>

<h2 id="choose-what-a-referenced-change-is-allowed-to-do">Choose what a referenced change is allowed to do</h2>
<p>A reference also needs an answer when its target changes. Our reservation’s customer and room are independently meaningful records, and cancelled reservations remain part of the retained agreement. We therefore chose <code>ON DELETE RESTRICT</code>: deleting either referenced record fails while reservations still refer to it. Changing its referenced key is restricted too.</p>
<p>Changing a reservation’s customer or room to another existing pair is different: its own foreign keys can pass. A room change must also satisfy the overlap rule. The database proves the new stored relationship is valid; it does not decide whether staff have permission to transfer the agreement.</p>
<table><caption>Reference actions follow ownership and retention</caption><thead><tr><th>Relationship</th><th>Possible decision</th><th>Change to test</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><td>Retained reservation → customer or room</td><td>Reject deletion; use an explicit lifecycle decision</td><td>Delete a customer with a cancelled reservation</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Disposable note → reservation</td><td>Cascade if the note has no independent retention duty</td><td>Delete one reservation; its notes disappear</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Optional referrer → customer</td><td>Set only the referrer ID to null if loss of that link is permitted</td><td>Delete the referrer while preserving the required tenant</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Reservation → replacement customer</td><td>Explicit reassignment, checked against all affected rules</td><td>Merge customers whose other identity rules conflict</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>Follow the whole cascade before choosing it. With customer → reservations → notes all cascading, deleting customer 7 removes reservations 17 and 18 and both sets of notes. It does not remove the rooms, which are referenced in the other direction. In the native test, one customer deletion removed both reservation branches. That may be correct for an owned draft document; it is a poor accidental retention policy for shared customers and completed agreements.</p>
<p><code>NO ACTION</code>, PostgreSQL’s default, rejects an unresolved reference when its check runs. A deferrable <code>NO ACTION</code> reference can allow a parent to be removed and its key restored before the transaction ends. <code>RESTRICT</code> refuses the referenced deletion or key change when attempted; it cannot be postponed that way. Cascading, nulling and default-setting actions perform their changes as part of the operation. Deferring a foreign key does not postpone those actions until commit.</p>
<p>Nulling must still satisfy required columns. PostgreSQL permits <code>ON DELETE SET NULL (referrer_id)</code> on a composite tenant/referrer reference, preserving the tenant rather than nulling both columns. A default-setting action also needs a valid resulting reference; it is not a way to bypass referential integrity.</p>
<p>Keep deletion authority separate from reference survival. An allowed cascade does not authorise an end user to invoke it, and a successful row deletion does not remove every retained copy. <a href="/review/relational-next/history/#specify-retention-and-erasure-beyond-the-row">History and data lifecycles</a> handles the retention contract; operations handles copies beyond live rows.</p>

<h2 id="account-for-rules-the-schema-cannot-express">Account for rules the schema cannot express</h2>
<p>Finish the design with an enforcement map. Each entry should identify the rule, the object that protects it, every relevant change path and a case that would expose a gap. This is more useful than saying that the database “has validation”.</p>
<table><caption>The final room-reservation contract</caption><thead><tr><th>Invariant</th><th>Enforcement</th><th>Changes and failure cases</th></tr></thead><tbody>
  <tr><td>Required, permitted values and a finite positive-duration interval</td><td><code>NOT NULL</code> and named row checks</td><td>Insert or update: nulls, unknown state, equal or reversed bounds, infinity</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Stable row identity; one current reference per tenant</td><td>Primary key; partial unique index</td><td>Insert, reference edit, reactivation and replacement; another current R17 fails</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Customer and room in the same tenant</td><td>Composite foreign keys, restricted parent changes</td><td>Insert, reassignment, tenant/key update, parent deletion</td></tr>
  <tr><td>A lead of this reservation, in the isolated group extension</td><td>Required lead ID and composite deferred reference; participant’s reverse reference</td><td>Complete creation passes; omitted or wrong-reservation lead fails at check time</td></tr>
  <tr><td>No overlapping confirmed use of a room</td><td>Predicate exclusion constraint plus interval validation</td><td>Insert, confirmation, room/tenant move and boundary change</td></tr>
  <tr><td>No more booked places than a class session’s capacity</td><td>Still an all-writers obligation in the parent design</td><td>Last place, reactivation, session move, capacity reduction</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>The last row is a different shape of rule. A PostgreSQL <code>CHECK</code> is not a maintained assertion over a count in another table. Hiding that query inside a function does not make the count rechecked whenever any relevant row changes.</p>
<p>There are three useful directions. Reshape the fact into separately identified places, with each booking claiming one unique place, if that matches the business and the cost of maintaining those places is acceptable. Write a database trigger that handles every insert, deletion, reactivation, move and capacity change. Or require all writers to use a transaction protocol protecting the count and the change together. A trigger centralises code, but still needs a concurrency argument: two sessions can otherwise each conclude that the last place is free. Restricting direct writes can make a chosen protocol enforceable across the application’s change paths.</p>
<p>Reshaping has limits too. A unique claim prevents two bookings using one place; it does not alone create the correct number of places, prevent an unauthorised extra place or make reducing capacity safe. Choose a representation for the whole requirement, including the changes it permits. <a href="/review/relational-next/normalisation/#give-every-derived-copy-a-maintenance-contract">Derived values need a maintenance contract</a> if a count is also stored.</p>
<p>Take four cases to <a href="/review/relational-next/transactions/">Making concurrent changes correct</a>: two current R17 creations may not both commit; overlapping confirmed reservations for the same room may not both commit; inserting a reservation during its parent’s deletion may not leave an orphan; mutually dependent creation may commit only with its final references satisfied. A wait, rejection or retry may be part of an acceptable execution. This article’s sequential tests establish stored-state acceptance, not the detailed scheduling of those competing sessions.</p>
<p>The remaining application duties are explicit: define legitimate state transitions and who can perform them, turn recognised constraint failures into useful responses, and protect rules not covered by the schema. When adding any of this to existing data, <a href="/review/relational-next/live-changes/">Changing a live database</a> begins with finding violations and planning compatible enforcement.</p>

